name: Mastodon work session - audit the shared controller base before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: ApplicationController
contract_file: app/controllers/application_controller.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 8 - a RE-QUESTION.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` at or below 0.50 AND sense
# at least +0.50 above it. FAR = the baseline cleared the question itself. NEAR = the
# baseline was held down but our arm did not reach either.
#
#   cycle 1  mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak   base 0.8125  sense 1.000  d +0.1875  FAR
#   cycle 2  mastodon.yaml.line-not-file.bak      base 0.158   sense 0.158  d  0.000   NEAR
#            (baseline half CLEARED; the sense arm never called blast on the class)
#   cycle 3  mastodon.yaml.table-altitude.bak     base 0.091   sense 0.091  d  0.000   NEAR
#            (both arms audited the accounts TABLE and agreed on the wrong question)
#   cycle 4  mastodon.yaml.names-the-class.bak    base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#   cycle 5  mastodon.yaml.not-the-call-form.bak  base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#   cycle 6  (author phase, `Account#local?`)     no run                              n/a
#            NO-ANCHOR reached from a per-row grep census - a screen used as a gate, which
#            laws.md forbids in any form. It decided nothing and is not counted.
#   cycle 7  mastodon.yaml.line-pin-no-shape.bak  base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#            Deliberately reproduced cycle 2's wording with only step 1 changed, aiming at
#            0.158. Landed at 1.000. Its own report: "cycle 2's 0.158 was the outlier, not
#            the rule, and it was n=1". The baseline hit its hard cap (304s of a 303s wall,
#            exit 124) and STILL had every row, `missed_cite: []`.
#
# SIX RUNS ON `Account`, FOUR AT OR ABOVE 0.81. WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM: the FAR
# side, and it does not correct by rewording. It moves the anchor, for a reason measured in
# the payload rather than argued from the trajectory - see THE ANCHOR MOVE below. Intended
# landing: baseline at or below ~0.40, sense at or above ~0.90.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ANCHOR MOVE - why `Account` was retired, quoted, not argued
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# laws.md says ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR, and that law is about not throwing
# away a measured contract when only the question failed. Three payload-level facts, run this
# cycle, say the remaining questions about `Account` are all the same question:
#
#   1. TOKEN COVERAGE OF THE SHOWN SET. Of the 57 files in the shown
#      `sense_blast {"symbol":"Account","file":"app/models/account.rb"}` payload, the number
#      that do NOT contain the literal token `Account` anywhere in the file is ZERO
#      (57 checked, 0 dark). Every row the tool can serve is a row a token grep can list.
#   2. NO SECOND HOP. The same blast at `max_hops: 2` returns `indirect_callers` = 0 entries
#      over 0 files. There is no transitive gold behind the direct callers.
#   3. NO RING. `ring_sweep.py --top 12` returns `0 candidate(s) above the bar`, all twelve
#      anchors at 0 rows / 0 files / 0 vias - so "what HOLDS X" is off the menu repo-wide.
#
# Together those bound what can be ASKED about `Account`, not merely what one wording did:
# any question about it resolves to a subset of one grep's file list. That is a property of
# the shown payload, which plan 01 requires every gold row to appear in - not a grep census
# and not a gate. Six benched cycles then measured what it costs: 0.81, 0.16, 0.09, 1.00,
# 1.00, 1.00.
#
# AND THE ONE REMAINING LEVER IS CLOSED BY THE SCORER. Cycles 5 and 7 both proposed "ask for
# a property the grep hit cannot settle - make the arm triage the candidates down". Read
# `bench/lib/scorer.py:832`: "NOTE: gold_f1 was DROPPED. Its precision punished Sense for
# completeness". The metric is recall only, so an arm that dumps every candidate pays nothing
# for over-citing, and any triage framing scores 1.00 by dumping. That lever cannot work.
#
# ONE LINE OUTSIDE THIS PHASE, per the scope lock. `gold.py:_cited` credits a gold path
# followed by ANY line number (`re.search(esc + r":\d+", hay)`); the specific line is never
# compared. So cycle 7's whole thesis - "the discriminator is which LINE, not which file" -
# was not measurable by the scorer as written. Not a defect in a decision script (the metric
# is file-pinned-to-a-line, by design) and not a STOPPER; naming it and carrying on. What it
# means for authoring is stated in THE AXIS: gold must be files the baseline cannot FIND.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ApplicationController` (app/controllers/application_controller.rb, 143 lines) is the base
# every web and API entry point in Mastodon inherits from, directly or through one of four
# intermediate bases. It declares the shared per-request surface - the current-actor and
# current-session lookups, the boolean query-parameter cast, the federation fetch-mode
# predicate, the single-user-instance predicate, the sign-out redirect and the functional-
# account gate. It is exactly the thing a maintainer reworking the request lifecycle opens
# first, and every controller in the tree is downstream of it.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, this cycle):
#   ApplicationController --file app/controllers/application_controller.rb
#     app caller files : 71   (total incl test/non-app: 71)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 30
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'ApplicationController' in 47 app files   PRECISION=1.51
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + grep-clean
#
# PRECISION 1.51 IS ABOVE 1.00 AND laws.md SAYS CHECK IT BY HAND BEFORE BELIEVING IT. Done,
# and it is NOT the false-edge case the law warns about - it is the inverse. Precision is
# dependents / grep hits, so above 1.00 means there are MORE real dependents than files
# carrying the token: 71 callers against 47 token files. Four samples opened by hand:
#   app/controllers/api/v1/statuses_controller.rb:3       class Api::V1::StatusesController < Api::BaseController
#   app/controllers/settings/imports_controller.rb:5      class Settings::ImportsController < Settings::BaseController
#   app/controllers/activitypub/outboxes_controller.rb:3  class ActivityPub::OutboxesController < ActivityPub::BaseController
#   app/controllers/api/v1/accounts/search_controller.rb:3 class Api::V1::Accounts::SearchController < Api::BaseController
# Real transitive-inheritance edges, and none of those four files writes the anchor token.
# Two OTHER anchors profiled this cycle DID turn out to be false edges and were rejected on
# a hand check, which is what the law asks for:
#   ApplicationPolicy   22 dark of 24 shown - REJECTED. app/controllers/about_controller.rb:8
#     is `expires_in(15.seconds, public: true, ...)` and home_controller.rb:6 is the same
#     line. Nothing to do with the policy. False edges.
#   AccountSerializer   22 dark of 40 shown - REJECTED. app/lib/feed_manager.rb contains no
#     match for `Serializer` or `serializ` at all (`grep -n` returns nothing). False edge.
# Ranked below this one - precision RANKS and never kills:
#   Status      0.60  (81 callers / 136 grep files, 35 dirs) - and it is this repo's BANKED
#               control cell (+0.53), so it is not available.
#   TagManager  0.53  (75 callers / 141 grep files, 22 dirs) - 0 of 45 shown files dark.
# Dark counts over the whole sweep: Setting 0/41, Adapter 0/30, Instance 0/19,
# ApplicationRecord 0/15, DeliveryWorker 1/32, Tag 7/44. `ApplicationController` at 46 dark
# of 52 shown is the only anchor swept whose shown payload carries twelve real rows that a
# grep on the anchor's token cannot list.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MECHANISM: a dependent reaches this contract by CALLING A METHOD IT INHERITED. The
# calling line names only the bare method - never the class that declares it, and never the
# intermediate base it came through. The bare name does not settle it either: the base mixes
# in nine separate modules and sits under an authentication library and an authorization
# library, all of which inject bare helper names into the same subclasses. So a dependent is
# settled by resolving WHICH declaration a bare call binds to, several inheritance hops up,
# past several mixins that could have supplied it.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS: it is the set of files
# that depend on the contract WITHOUT EVER WRITING ITS NAME. 46 of the 52 files in the shown
# blast payload contain no occurrence of `ApplicationController` at all, and all 19 scoring
# rows below are drawn from those 46. The answer is an attribution - which bare, shadowed
# name resolves to this base - and the anchor's own token is absent from every scoring row.
# This is the first draft in eight cycles that is not KIND ONE.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY
# FORM): the token sits in 47 app files against 71 real caller files. Nothing here was
# decided by a grep; the two-arm run in the next phase decides. The honest risk, stated
# rather than screened: a baseline that opens the base class first can read the method names
# off it and grep each one. What it then has to do, and what this axis measures, is separate
# the methods this base declares from the ones its nine mixins and two libraries supply.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN (bench/lib/mcp_probe.py, this cycle, no min_confidence passed):
#   sense_blast {"symbol":"ApplicationController","file":"app/controllers/application_controller.rb"}
#     -> risk_factors ["180 direct callers"], 52 caller files shown over 30 areas.
# EVERY one of the 19 `dependents` rows below appears in that shown payload, confirmed by
# `gold_confidence_check.py --group dependents`: "gold rows: 19  shown: 19  missing: 0". The payload
# names the enclosing `def`, not the dependency line, so every line below was opened and read
# by hand this cycle; the relations are written from that output, never from a script tally.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, this cycle):
#   recall 0.30, hits_n 3, truth_n 10, samples [0.0, 0.3, 0.0]
#   reason: "recall 0.3 clears 0.3 but only 3 member(s) named (< 5) - generic framework
#            vocabulary, not recitation of THIS symbol"
#   hits: current_account, current_session, sso_account_settings
# Not recited as a class. But two of the three recited names are real members, so a model may
# reach for them WITHOUT reading the file. Acted on rather than noted: the gold group is
# deliberately weighted AWAY from them - 9 rows depend on the boolean query-param cast, 1 on
# the federation fetch-mode predicate, 1 on the single-user predicate; only 7 turn on the
# recited current-actor lookup and 1 on the recited session lookup. No gold row's credit is a
# member list; each is a line-level fact at a path the probe never produces.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the shared request-handling base that every web
  and API entry point in this codebase inherits from - the per-request helpers it
  declares for its subclasses to use. Before you touch it, audit what depends on that
  base, completely enough that the rework cannot silently break a dependent. For each
  one give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase on how
  it uses the base. A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed
  dependent is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the shared controller base contract
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, name the
    single base class this ticket is about, say where it is defined, and say what a rework
    of it would have to preserve. Settle on the base as a whole and on what it declares for
    its subclasses to use - the audit that follows is about everything downstream of it,
    not about any one part of it and not about how requests are routed to it. Give
    file:line for everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the shared controller base
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    depends on the shared base, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be exhaustive and
    group them by area. Nearly all of these dependents never write the base's name anywhere
    in their file: they take the dependency by calling something they inherited, so the
    calling line names only a bare method and says nothing about where that method was
    declared. The bare name does not settle it either - the base has several modules mixed
    into it and sits under libraries that inject helper names of their own into the very
    same subclasses, and the subclasses reach the base through intermediate bases rather
    than directly. Deciding that a bare call really does land on this base is the work. For
    each dependent give file:line and one phrase on how it uses the base. A filename with
    no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Named dependents that never write the base class name
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the shared controller base. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (5, NON-SCORING anchors): the base itself and the four intermediate bases
#     that inherit from it. Both arms reach these - they are the files that write the
#     anchor's name in an inheritance position - and they do NOT score. They are here so the
#     scored group is not diluted by the obvious centre.
#   - `dependents` (19, THE DISCRIMINATOR): one row per FILE, across 14 distinct directories.
#     Every row appears in the shown blast payload above. NOT ONE of the 19 files contains
#     the string `ApplicationController` anywhere - checked file by file this cycle, 19 of 19
#     dark - so no grep on the anchor's token can list them.
# Method spread, deliberate, so no single bare-name grep covers the group and so the two
# recited names carry as little of it as possible: boolean query-param cast 9, current-actor
# lookup 7, federation fetch-mode predicate 1, current-session lookup 1, single-user-instance
# predicate 1.
gold:
- {id: contract-base, group: contract, match: [app/controllers/application_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/application_controller.rb:3 `class ApplicationController < ActionController::Base` - the contract under rework; declares the shared per-request helper surface at lines 103-129"}
- {id: c:api-base, group: contract, match: [app/controllers/api/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/base_controller.rb:3 `class Api::BaseController < ApplicationController` - intermediate base every API controller inherits through"}
- {id: c:admin-base, group: contract, match: [app/controllers/admin/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/base_controller.rb:4 `class BaseController < ApplicationController` - intermediate base for the admin tree"}
- {id: c:settings-base, group: contract, match: [app/controllers/settings/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/settings/base_controller.rb:3 `class Settings::BaseController < ApplicationController` - intermediate base for the user-settings tree"}
- {id: c:activitypub-base, group: contract, match: [app/controllers/activitypub/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/base_controller.rb:3 `class ActivityPub::BaseController < Api::BaseController` - second-hop base, reaches the contract through the API base"}
- {id: d:outbox-page-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/activitypub/outboxes_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/outboxes_controller.rb:68 `truthy_param?(:page)` - casts the federation outbox paging flag with the base's boolean query-param helper"}
- {id: d:replies-only-other, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/activitypub/replies_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/replies_controller.rb:61 `truthy_param?(:only_other_accounts)` - casts the reply-collection filter flag with the base's boolean query-param helper"}
- {id: d:context-fetch-mode, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/activitypub/contexts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/contexts_controller.rb:18 `expires_in 3.minutes, public: public_fetch_mode?` - sets cache visibility from the base's federation fetch-mode predicate"}
- {id: d:directory-local-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v1/directories_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/directories_controller.rb:34 `truthy_param?(:local)` - decides local-only directory listing through the base's boolean query-param helper"}
- {id: d:relationship-suspended-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v1/accounts/relationships_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/accounts/relationships_controller.rb:9 `@accounts.merge!(Account.without_suspended) unless truthy_param?(:with_suspended)` - the base's cast decides whether suspended rows are filtered out of the query"}
- {id: d:follow-reblogs-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v1/accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/accounts_controller.rb:48 `reblogs: params.key?(:reblogs) ? truthy_param?(:reblogs) : nil` - two follow options cast through the base's boolean query-param helper"}
- {id: d:status-delete-media-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v1/statuses_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses_controller.rb:88 `RemovalWorker.perform_async(@status.id, { 'redraft' => !truthy_param?(:delete_media) })` - the base's cast decides the redraft flag handed to a background worker"}
- {id: d:public-timeline-auth, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v1/timelines/public_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/timelines/public_controller.rb:18 `if truthy_param?(:local)` - the base's cast selects which live-feed access setting gates the request"}
- {id: d:search-resolve-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v2/search_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v2/search_controller.rb:76 `resolve: truthy_param?(:resolve),` - three search options cast through the base's boolean query-param helper"}
- {id: d:notification-filter-param, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v2/notifications_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v2/notifications_controller.rb:112 `include_filtered: truthy_param?(:include_filtered)` - the base's cast widens the notification scope built from the current actor"}
- {id: d:push-subscription-session, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/web/push_subscriptions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/web/push_subscriptions_controller.rb:30 `@active_session ||= current_session` - memoises the base's current-session lookup as the subscription owner"}
- {id: d:session-single-user-path, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/auth/sessions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/auth/sessions_controller.rb:96 `paths << short_account_path(username: resource.account) if single_user_mode? && resource.is_a?(User)` - post-login redirect targets branch on the base's single-user-instance predicate"}
- {id: d:appeal-reject-actor, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/admin/disputes/appeals_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/disputes/appeals_controller.rb:23 `@appeal.reject!(current_account)` - records the base's current actor as the moderator who rejected the appeal"}
- {id: d:report-action-params, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/admin/reports/actions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/reports/actions_controller.rb:39 `current_account: current_account,` - passes the base's current actor into the moderation-action parameter hash"}
- {id: d:trend-link-batch, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/admin/trends/links_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/trends/links_controller.rb:15 `Trends::PreviewCardBatch.new(trends_preview_card_batch_params.merge(current_account: current_account, action: action_from_button))` - the base's current actor is merged in as the acting moderator on the batch form"}
- {id: d:import-form-owner, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/settings/imports_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/settings/imports_controller.rb:31 `@import = Form::Import.new(current_account: current_account)` - constructs the import form around the base's current actor"}
- {id: d:oauth-userinfo-subject, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/oauth/userinfo_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/oauth/userinfo_controller.rb:8 `@account = current_account` - the OIDC userinfo document is serialized from the base's current actor"}
- {id: d:filter-keyword-scope, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/api/v2/filters/keywords_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v2/filters/keywords_controller.rb:44 `CustomFilterKeyword.includes(:custom_filter).where(custom_filter: { account: current_account }).find(params[:id])` - scopes the keyword lookup to the base's current actor"}
- {id: d:initial-state-privacy, group: dependents, match: [app/helpers/application_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/application_helper.rb:217 `default_privacy = current_account&.user&.setting_default_privacy` - a view helper, not a controller, reading the base's current actor through the helper_method export at line 19"}
